## 4. User stories and acceptance criteria
**Story 1 (FR01):** As a resident, I want to filter workshops by topic and date, so that I can find workshops that suit me.
AC1: Given workshops on different topics, when I choose a topic, only workshops on that topic are shown.
AC2: When I choose a date, only workshops on that date are shown.

**Story 2 FR03:** As a resident, I want to request one place on a workshop, so that I can attend it
AC1: When I submit my name and contact email, the request is recorded
AC2: The screen says the request was sent and that it is not a confirmed place.

**Story 3 (FR04):** As an organiser, I want to approve or reject a request, so that I decide who gets a place.
AC1: I can see the pending requests for a workshop.
AC2: When I approve or reject, the decision is recorded with the request

**AC vs DoD:** Acceptance criteria are conditions for one backlog item. The Definition of Done is a shared quality standard for every Increment.

## 5. Product Backlog
Order | Item | Status | Reason 

1 | View and filter workshops (FR01, FR02) | Supported | Residents cannot request a place without finding a workshop 
2 | Request one place (FR03) | Supported | This is the main goal of a resident 
3 | Approve or reject a request (FR04) | Supported | The scenario says organisers decide 
4 | Maintain workshop details (FR05) | Supported | Keeps workshop information correct 
5 | Group reservations | Candidate | Not agreed 
6 | Cancellation arrangements | Candidate | Not agreed 
7 | Automated reminders | Candidate | Not agreed 

**First two choices:** Items 1 and 2 are the core service. Without them nothing else has value.

**Product Owner and policy:** The Product Owner orders the backlog. The centre manager confirms policies, so the Product Owner cannot decide group, cancellation or reminder rules alone.

## 6. Sprint simulation
**Sprint Goal:** Residents can find workshops and request one place

**Sprint Backlog:** FR01, FR02, FR03
Plan: show the workshop list; add the topic and date filter; add the request form; record the request; check the results.

**Definition of Done (example):** The search is tested, works with the keyboard, has labelled fields and has no severe defects

**Sprint Review:** A stakeholder suggests group booking. I record it as a candidate. Questions before any decision:
1. How many places can one request ask for?
2. Who approves the group booking rule? centre manager

## 7. Own project Volunteer and Event Coordination
**Story A (FR06):** As a volunteer, I want to view events that need volunteers, so that I know where help is needed.
AC: Given events exist, when I open the event list, I see the events that need volunteers

**Story B (FR07):** As a volunteer, I want to apply for a role in an event, so that the organiser can consider me.
AC1: When I submit an application, it is recorded.
AC2: The screen says it is not a confirmed place

